---
import HelpLayout from '../../layouts/HelpLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';

/**
 * Sibling of fitnotes-alternative.astro and strong-alternative.astro. Read the
 * FitNotes header first; the trademark and source-checking rules are identical,
 * and the no-claims-about-their-pricing rule from the Strong page applies here
 * too.
 *
 * Import claims checked against hevy_parser.dart and hevy_importer.dart on
 * 2026-09-05:
 *   - weight unit from the weight_kg / weight_lbs header, distance from
 *     distance_km / distance_miles (hevy_parser.dart:104-111)
 *   - carried: title, exercise_title, reps, duration_seconds, exercise_notes,
 *     set_index, rpe (importer :253), and set_type, which is why warmups
 *     survive as warmups (:197, importer :252)
 *   - real session start/end from start_time / end_time (:90-92)
 *   - superset_id IS parsed (:198) but the importer does NOT rebuild supersets
 *     from it. Do not claim supersets come across. If that changes, this
 *     comment and the page both need updating.
 */

const description =
  'Move to Badger from Hevy and keep the detail. The CSV is the richest of the lot: warmups arrive as warmups, RPE comes with them, and your log stops needing an account.';
---

<HelpLayout title="Moving from Hevy" description={description}>
  <ArticleSchema
    slot="head"
    headline="Moving to Badger from Hevy"
    description={description}
    dateModified="2026-09-24"
    about={['Hevy', 'Workout tracking']}
    breadcrumb={[
      { name: 'Guides', url: '/guides/' },
      { name: 'Switching to Badger', url: '/guides/switching/' },
    ]}
  />

  <p class="crumb"><a href="/guides/switching/">Switching to Badger</a></p>

  <h1>Moving to Badger from Hevy</h1>

  <p>
    If you want the same log without an account behind it or a subscription on top, your Hevy export
    comes with you. Here is what it brings across and what changes.
  </p>

  <h2 id="import">What the CSV carries across</h2>

  <p>
    Hevy's CSV is the most detailed of the exports Badger reads, so after a FitNotes backup it is the
    one that brings the most across.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Warmups stay warmups.</strong> Hevy is the only app Badger imports from whose export
      marks set type, so your ramp-up sets arrive flagged and stay out of your PRs and volume.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Every set</strong>, with its date, exercise, weight, reps, distance, and time.</li>
    <li><strong>Your RPE</strong>, on each set.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your exercise notes</strong>, on the exercise they belong to, and each workout's title
      as a note on that session.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Session times.</strong> Imported workouts show when they started and how long they ran.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Kilograms or pounds, kilometers or miles</strong>, read from the file's column names and
      kept in the unit you logged.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your PRs</strong>, worked out from the imported history, so your records and graphs
      start out full.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>What does not come across:</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Supersets, as supersets.</strong> All the sets arrive, but Badger does not rebuild the
      grouping yet.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your routines.</strong> The export holds history only. Start from one of Badger's ten
      built-in programs or build your own.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your folders and categories.</strong> New exercises land in an "Imported" category for
      you to sort. An exercise already in your Badger library is matched by name.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="how">Doing it</h2>

  <p>
    In Hevy, go to <strong>Profile &rarr; Settings &rarr; Export Data &rarr; Export as CSV</strong>,
    move the file to the phone you train with, then open <strong>Settings &rarr; Your data &rarr;
    Import from Hevy</strong> in Badger. Import each file once. The full steps are in
    <a href="/help/getting-started/#importing">importing from another app</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-you-get">What you get, and what you give up</h2>

  <p><strong>What you get:</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>No account.</strong> Your log lives on your phone, with nothing to sign in to.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No paywall.</strong> No cap on routines, history or graphs, and exporting is free.
      <a href="/guides/workout-tracker-no-subscription/">The no-subscription guide</a> compares the
      trackers people usually weigh up.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Gym and machine tracking</strong>, with PRs kept per machine.
      <a href="/help/gyms/">How it works</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>More per-exercise analysis</strong>: estimated 1RM, eleven graphs per exercise, muscle
      balance and plateau detection. <a href="/help/progress/">What it tracks</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>What you give up:</strong></p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The social side.</strong> No feed, no followers, no shared workouts. If that is what
      keeps you training, Badger is the wrong move.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sync between devices.</strong> Your log lives on one phone. Moving to a new one means
      carrying a backup file across: <a href="/help/phone-migration/">moving to a new phone</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="getting-out">Getting back out again</h2>

  <p>
    Leaving is as easy as arriving. Every set exports to CSV, and the whole database backs up to a
    file you keep. Routines, exercises and equipment share as <code>.badger</code> files, a
    <a href="/badger-format/">documented format</a> you can read in any text editor.
  </p>

  <h2 id="trademark">About the name</h2>

  <p class="fineprint">
    Hevy is a trademark of its owner, and Badger is not affiliated with, endorsed by, sponsored by, or
    connected to it in any way. This page describes compatibility only: Badger reads the CSV file that
    Hevy exports.
  </p>
</HelpLayout>

<style>
  .crumb {
    margin: 0 0 6px;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .fineprint {
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }
</style>
